#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116425 :tutorial/content "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time. The rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},$$ where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is $$(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.$$ Since $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is $$\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.$$ So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\ & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\ & =f(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft})\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "be8f798d-68e4-549b-a2c6-ce3fd435c044" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14562 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116426 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant. A solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$ represents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is $$450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is $$\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\ & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\ & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\ & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} \\\\ & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}$$ which finally gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b84bba1-b479-506a-8e25-77650b293eee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14563 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116427 :tutorial/content "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$V(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, $$V(t) = V_0.$$\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}$$ Solving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}$$ Solving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c551e08-3014-52f3-83f1-73ec27aa2e82" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14564 :tutorial/title "Points of Overflow and Emptying"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116428 :tutorial/content "So far, we have seen that if a container initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance, and\n\n- a solution with concentration $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ flows into the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ while\n- the completely mixed solution flows out at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min},$\n\nthen the amount $Q(t)$ of the substance in the container satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = be.$$\n\nFor the rest of this lesson, we'll focus on the special case where the input solution contains *no substance*, that is, where $b=0.$ In this case, the right-hand side vanishes, and the differential equation becomes *separable*:\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac1Q\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = - \\dfrac{f}{V_0 + (e-f)t}$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is negative, meaning the amount of substance in the container decreases over time. We can now solve the differential equation directly by separation of variables.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider our previous example: a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, and a solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nWe found that the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank after $t$ minutes is modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{4}{225}Q = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{4}{225}.$$\n\nWe solve this equation by integrating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}\\,dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{225})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{4}{225}\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|Q| & =-\\frac{4t}{225}+C \\\\ Q & ={e}^{-4t/225+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-4t/225}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote that $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nFinally, since the tank initially contains $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-4(0)/225} & =6 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{Q(t) = 6e^{-4t/225}}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a4e43cc-21fe-5b6e-ab72-10aef2431693" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14654 :tutorial/title "Solving a Dilution Model Where the Input Has a Concentration of Zero"}]}
